WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.605
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy and this is mCoding.

00:00:03.605 --> 00:00:08.865
Today, we're learning about the crazy code behind Python's itertools permutations function.

00:00:08.865 --> 00:00:11.837
What permutations does is pretty straightforward.

00:00:11.837 --> 00:00:14.862
It gives you all of the permutations of an&nbsp;object.

00:00:14.862 --> 00:00:17.237
The second parameter allows you to get partial permutations,

00:00:17.237 --> 00:00:21.204
or sequences from the&nbsp;original set taken without replacement.

00:00:21.204 --> 00:00:22.275
Using it is straightforward.

00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:24.321
Just give it the items that you&nbsp;want permutations of

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:27.466
and the length of the partial permutations that you want.

00:00:27.466 --> 00:00:31.467
Looping over the&nbsp;results, we see that this is a pretty easy way to get all the permutations.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:34.574
But have you ever thought&nbsp;about how you might implement a function like this?

00:00:34.574 --> 00:00:37.911
Well, a viewer of mine, Jerry, was thinking about&nbsp;this.

00:00:37.911 --> 00:00:41.904
Jerry sent in the following code that he found in the Python docs.

00:00:41.904 --> 00:00:44.065
Technically, you're&nbsp;probably using CPython.

00:00:44.065 --> 00:00:47.806
And in CPython, this function is actually written in C.

00:00:47.806 --> 00:00:49.689
But it still&nbsp;does essentially the same thing.

00:00:49.689 --> 00:00:52.572
So, this is just the Python version explaining how it works.

00:00:52.572 --> 00:00:56.816
But wow.&nbsp;This code looks like an absolute mess. What's going on here?

00:00:56.816 --> 00:01:00.365
We've got conditional breaking, mutating&nbsp;objects as we're looping

00:01:00.365 --> 00:01:03.766
and branching inside of a for-loop inside of a while-loop.

00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:08.792
Not to mention,&nbsp;we're also using the somewhat obscure in Python for-else paradigm.

00:01:08.792 --> 00:01:10.173
If I saw this in a real codebase,

00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:14.058
I'd half expect it to have a comment saying: BLACK MAGIC, DO NOT EDIT.

00:01:14.058 --> 00:01:18.868
This is the kind of code&nbsp;that looks so complicated that it scares people away from understanding it.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:21.984
And before my viewer&nbsp;wrote in, that's exactly what happened to me.

00:01:21.984 --> 00:01:24.475
I've seen the documentation for itertools&nbsp;before.

00:01:24.475 --> 00:01:26.271
I've read this code before.

00:01:26.271 --> 00:01:27.478
But after one glance,

00:01:27.478 --> 00:01:30.860
I decided that it was probably some&nbsp;clever mathematician that came up with the algorithm.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:35.520
And there's a 20 page proof attached&nbsp;to it explaining why it does the right thing.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.785
But I claim that the underlying idea is actually&nbsp;very simple.

00:01:38.785 --> 00:01:42.322
And by the end of this video, you too are going to understand it.

00:01:42.322 --> 00:01:45.729
Surprisingly enough,&nbsp;the key to understanding this code

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:49.072
is to not try to understand the code.

00:01:49.072 --> 00:01:52.680
What I mean is, don't try&nbsp;to figure out what all these things are doing.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.622
Instead, try to just solve the problem.

00:01:55.622 --> 00:01:59.847
If you&nbsp;had to come up with a way to iterate over all the permutations of some list,

00:01:59.847 --> 00:02:01.131
how would you do&nbsp;it?

00:02:01.131 --> 00:02:07.112
I asked this because the itertools code is using probably the simplest algorithm that you&nbsp;could come up with.

00:02:07.112 --> 00:02:09.800
Optimized into oblivion.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.570
And by starting with the simple algorithm, you too can&nbsp;get to this one.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.306
So, let's start from the ground up.

00:02:16.306 --> 00:02:21.520
The simplest version of this problem is to&nbsp;just count how many permutations are there.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.188
Pick the first element of your permutation. That's&nbsp;n choices.

00:02:25.188 --> 00:02:28.346
For the second element, you've got n - 1 choices

00:02:28.346 --> 00:02:30.724
And so on until you get to the&nbsp;r-th element.

00:02:30.724 --> 00:02:32.366
Then we just multiply the numbers together.

00:02:32.366 --> 00:02:37.280
So, we start with n * (n - 1) * all the way down to (n - r + 1).

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.253
If r was equal to n, which is the case of&nbsp;full permutations,

00:02:40.253 --> 00:02:42.080
then this gives n factorial.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.481
But just for example, suppose we have 5 things&nbsp;and we want 3 permutations,

00:02:45.481 --> 00:02:49.240
then we have 5 choices for the first thing, 4 choices for the&nbsp;second thing

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.200
and 3 choices for the third thing.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.869
This is a very straightforward counting argument.

00:02:53.869 --> 00:02:55.669
You can implement this using reduce.

00:02:55.669 --> 00:02:59.048
Or just using a for-loop, you can multiply the things together.

00:02:59.048 --> 00:03:04.013
The next step is to take our counting procedure and turn that into a recursive algorithm.

00:03:04.013 --> 00:03:06.253
Well,&nbsp;we started counting by picking an element.

00:03:06.253 --> 00:03:09.417
Okay. So, suppose that we pick the first element.

00:03:09.417 --> 00:03:11.627
Well, if we want to find a permutation of length r

00:03:11.627 --> 00:03:15.320
and we've fixed the first element being 0,&nbsp;in this case,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.880
then what's left to do to find all the permutations that start with 0

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.162
is to just&nbsp;find all the permutations of length r - 1 from the remaining items.

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:27.586
But that's exactly the same&nbsp;question just on a smaller input.

00:03:27.586 --> 00:03:31.086
We can just keep track of what part of our permutation is fixed

00:03:31.086 --> 00:03:33.643
and what part remains to be enumerated.

00:03:33.643 --> 00:03:35.821
Each time we recurse we fix another element.

00:03:35.821 --> 00:03:37.637
And eventually, we fixed our elements

00:03:37.637 --> 00:03:40.452
which is the length of the permutation that we're looking for.

00:03:40.452 --> 00:03:43.504
Let's take a&nbsp;stab at implementing this recursive structure.

00:03:43.504 --> 00:03:44.737
Like many recursive problems,

00:03:44.737 --> 00:03:48.047
the actual recursion will&nbsp;be implemented inside some helper function

00:03:48.047 --> 00:03:51.615
that has some additional information that we don't&nbsp;want to expose to the user.

00:03:51.615 --> 00:03:54.304
In this case, the additional information is this variable i

00:03:54.304 --> 00:03:58.178
which&nbsp;tells us the number of things in the permutation that have already been fixed.

00:03:58.178 --> 00:04:00.751
At this point, don't&nbsp;worry about input validation or anything like that.

00:04:00.751 --> 00:04:04.695
And just assume that items is a list from 0 to&nbsp;n - 1.

00:04:04.695 --> 00:04:06.579
Remember, our function is a generator.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.599
It runs and then it pauses and can yield a value.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:12.240
Each time we have a new permutation, we'll yield.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.498
Then the caller can just look at the current state&nbsp;of the items to determine the permutation.

00:04:16.498 --> 00:04:20.800
This is just an implementation detail and we can hide&nbsp;that from the user by doing something like this.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.390
Every time our recursive generator yields, we just&nbsp;make a copy of the first r items and that's our&nbsp;permutation.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.074
First up, our base case.

00:04:28.074 --> 00:04:30.496
r is the number&nbsp;of remaining things left to choose.

00:04:30.496 --> 00:04:33.397
If there are no more things left to choose, then we're done.

00:04:33.397 --> 00:04:35.720
Yield&nbsp;the current permutation and stop.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.418
If you're not sure why the yield should be there,

00:04:37.418 --> 00:04:41.860
then consider&nbsp;the case where someone asks for length 0 sequences.

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:45.356
How many zero length permutations of n items&nbsp;are there?

00:04:45.356 --> 00:04:48.000
Well, just one. The empty permutation.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.940
Then the recursive part of the algorithm&nbsp;looks like this.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.459
We want to say for each of the remaining items, fix that item and then recurse.

00:04:55.459 --> 00:04:59.696
So, if the fixed portion of our permutation is just 0 so far,

00:04:59.696 --> 00:05:01.817
then we want to fix 1 and recurse

00:05:01.817 --> 00:05:03.682
and&nbsp;then fix 2 and recurse

00:05:03.682 --> 00:05:06.485
and then fix 3 in recurse and so on.

00:05:06.485 --> 00:05:10.218
The location just after the bar that&nbsp;determines the things that haven't yet been fixed

00:05:10.218 --> 00:05:13.072
is exactly what our local variable i represents.

00:05:13.072 --> 00:05:15.840
So, this loop should be a loop from i to the end.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.000
In order to fix an item, we just move it into&nbsp;the i-th position.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.459
The way we do that is just by swapping the i and j entries in the list.

00:05:23.459 --> 00:05:26.720
Notice&nbsp;what happens when we swap i and j entries repeatedly.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.526
From here to here, we swap 0 and 1.

00:05:29.526 --> 00:05:33.360
Then we swap 0&nbsp;and 2 to get to here. Then 0 and 3 to get to here.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.000
Notice that each iteration puts the element

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.268
that was moved in the previous iteration&nbsp;back in its original location except off by&nbsp;one.

00:05:42.268 --> 00:05:44.798
So, after iteration 0, the 0 moved to 1.

00:05:44.798 --> 00:05:47.135
After iteration 1, the 1 moved to 2.

00:05:47.135 --> 00:05:50.320
After iteration 2, the 2 moved to 3.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.604
After the last iteration, we just have n - 1 in the fixed slot

00:05:53.604 --> 00:05:57.713
And then all the numbers in their original order just shifted over&nbsp;by 1.

00:05:57.713 --> 00:05:59.485
This is actually a slight problem.

00:05:59.485 --> 00:06:03.910
After swapping an item into the correct place, we&nbsp;would like to just make the recursive call.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.544
The recursive call is pretty straightforward.

00:06:05.544 --> 00:06:09.166
We just&nbsp;say one more thing is fixed and one less thing needs to be fixed.

00:06:09.166 --> 00:06:10.862
But remember, I'm not making&nbsp;copies here.

00:06:10.862 --> 00:06:13.935
So, the recursive call might modify items.

00:06:13.935 --> 00:06:18.191
If items is getting scrambled, then how do&nbsp;I know that I'm choosing each element one time?

00:06:18.191 --> 00:06:21.060
Say, I swapped 0 and 2 and then made a recursive&nbsp;call.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:24.674
If all the numbers got jumbled then when I go to swap 0 and 3,

00:06:24.674 --> 00:06:26.919
the 3 might not be in&nbsp;that position anymore.

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:31.757
Well, the great thing about recursion is that you can assume that a&nbsp;recursive call has some nice property

00:06:31.757 --> 00:06:36.666
And then use that property as long as you're willing&nbsp;to maintain that property in the current call.

00:06:36.666 --> 00:06:38.587
So, what I can do is add the restriction that

00:06:38.587 --> 00:06:41.931
after&nbsp;I yield all of the items from the recursive call,

00:06:41.931 --> 00:06:46.623
the items are going to be put back in the exact&nbsp;same position that they were in before the call.

00:06:46.623 --> 00:06:49.417
If I have 0, 1, 2, 3 and so on in order,

00:06:49.417 --> 00:06:51.601
I can swap&nbsp;in the next position,

00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:53.860
make a recursive call, iterate over all the results.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.183
And after the iteration is&nbsp;done, I'm in the same order.

00:06:57.183 --> 00:06:59.911
So, from the view of the caller, at this point in the code,

00:06:59.911 --> 00:07:03.659
items has the&nbsp;exact same state as it did right before the for-loop.

00:07:03.659 --> 00:07:06.959
That's very convenient for me. But now I have&nbsp;to maintain that property.

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:11.503
After I swap 0 1, 0 2, 0 3 and so on, we end up in this state.

00:07:11.503 --> 00:07:16.405
We have the&nbsp;last element at the position just before the bar followed by the rest of them in order.

00:07:16.405 --> 00:07:19.252
In order to&nbsp;maintain the invariant and get back the original order,

00:07:19.252 --> 00:07:23.093
I just have to take the item just before the&nbsp;bar and push it to the end.

00:07:23.093 --> 00:07:26.521
Everything else slides left one and we're back exactly where we started.

00:07:26.521 --> 00:07:30.213
That means, here after the loop, we need to push the i-th element to the back of the list.

00:07:30.213 --> 00:07:33.542
My preferred&nbsp;way to do that is to just pop the i-th element and append it.

00:07:33.542 --> 00:07:38.562
Let's quickly fix this error. That's&nbsp;supposed to be the first r items not the last r items.

00:07:38.562 --> 00:07:39.867
And then we should be good to go.

00:07:39.867 --> 00:07:43.530
And there&nbsp;you have it, printing out the 3 permutations of 5 elements.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.943
You can compare against the itertools output. They're the same.

00:07:45.943 --> 00:07:49.717
So, let's get rid of these unnecessary comments and just take a look.

00:07:49.717 --> 00:07:51.971
Now, I get it. if you're not that familiar with recursion,

00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:54.416
then this could still be a bit tricky to&nbsp;understand.

00:07:54.416 --> 00:07:58.259
But if you know the basics of recursion, this is one of the simpler recursive algorithms.

00:07:58.259 --> 00:08:02.593
It's a pretty straightforward. Just do a base case, loop over some remaining choices

00:08:02.593 --> 00:08:06.400
and then do the&nbsp;recursive problem and then restore an invariant.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.699
If you're familiar with recursion,

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:11.415
this really is&nbsp;one of the first algorithms that you'd come up with to solve this problem.

00:08:11.415 --> 00:08:14.445
And in my opinion, it's&nbsp;actually quite readable for what it does.

00:08:14.445 --> 00:08:16.018
It reads like the counting argument.

00:08:16.018 --> 00:08:20.307
Try all the choices, fix&nbsp;that choice and then find the smaller permutations.

00:08:20.307 --> 00:08:22.301
If this was the algorithm in the itertools docs,

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:24.705
I don't think that anyone would have emailed me about it.

00:08:24.705 --> 00:08:27.100
So, why doesn't itertools use this&nbsp;approach?

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:31.493
What if I want just the first few permutations of a really long list.

00:08:31.493 --> 00:08:33.490
Say, I take 0 to&nbsp;1999.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:37.732
There are 2000 factorial permutations which is way too many to fit in memory.

00:08:37.732 --> 00:08:40.885
But itertools has&nbsp;no problem listing off the first hundred of them.

00:08:40.885 --> 00:08:42.902
Let's see what goes wrong with our version.

00:08:42.902 --> 00:08:44.940
In our version, we get a recursion error.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.431
And this makes complete sense. What's the depth of&nbsp;our recursive call stack?

00:08:48.431 --> 00:08:51.216
We decrease r by 1 with every recursive call.

00:08:51.216 --> 00:08:53.733
So, we make approximately r&nbsp;recursive calls.

00:08:53.733 --> 00:08:58.474
We can find out how many function calls we can have on the stack by printing&nbsp;out the recursion limit.

00:08:58.474 --> 00:09:01.209
Technically, all functions count towards this, not just recursive&nbsp;calls.

00:09:01.209 --> 00:09:05.372
So, it should be called something like 'get function stack limit'.

00:09:05.372 --> 00:09:06.963
But we're just being&nbsp;approximate anyway.

00:09:06.963 --> 00:09:10.000
For me, the recursion limit is a very small 1000.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.780
You can change the recursion&nbsp;limit with this call.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.901
But in this case, that still doesn't work.

00:09:13.901 --> 00:09:17.548
It ran for a while and then crashed&nbsp;because Python's stack isn't big enough.

00:09:17.548 --> 00:09:19.826
Yeah, you can increase the stack size a little bit.

00:09:19.826 --> 00:09:22.271
But this&nbsp;still is just not the right approach.

00:09:22.271 --> 00:09:25.766
If we want to support really large values of r, then we have no&nbsp;choice.

00:09:25.766 --> 00:09:28.010
Recursion is out of the question.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:29.579
So, how do you take a recursive algorithm

00:09:29.579 --> 00:09:33.423
and change it into&nbsp;a not recursive algorithm, an iterative algorithm?

00:09:33.423 --> 00:09:38.144
Well, keep in mind at the level of your hardware,&nbsp;there is no such thing as recursion.

00:09:38.144 --> 00:09:41.615
Everything at the level of machine code is iterative.

00:09:41.615 --> 00:09:45.889
So, there's&nbsp;some kind of automatic process that's happening that's getting rid of the recursion.

00:09:45.889 --> 00:09:48.498
You're running&nbsp;some code. When you make a recursive call,

00:09:48.498 --> 00:09:51.527
you put another function call onto the function stack.

00:09:51.527 --> 00:09:55.434
The function stack is a data structure that your computer is maintaining for you.

00:09:55.434 --> 00:10:00.199
So, the general&nbsp;purpose way to get around this problem is to use your own stack.

00:10:00.199 --> 00:10:02.096
Function stack space is very&nbsp;limited.

00:10:02.096 --> 00:10:03.155
But if you make your own stack,

00:10:03.155 --> 00:10:06.149
you can make it as big as you want, up to your computer's&nbsp;memory limit.

00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:09.271
But this video is already too long. So, we're not going to take that approach.

00:10:09.271 --> 00:10:13.288
Instead,&nbsp;we're going to take the much less general approach of trying to be clever.

00:10:13.288 --> 00:10:19.005
And yes, person-already-typing-in-the-comments, I too agree that you shouldn't try to be clever.

00:10:19.005 --> 00:10:23.548
But I guarantee you,&nbsp;this code was created by someone being clever.

00:10:23.548 --> 00:10:26.230
Here's how we'd be clever with the recursive&nbsp;function.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.849
The goal is to eliminate all the recursive calls.

00:10:28.849 --> 00:10:31.712
The only outside observable&nbsp;property of this function is that

00:10:31.712 --> 00:10:35.631
it yields at the right times and then it swaps the elements in the&nbsp;right order.

00:10:35.631 --> 00:10:39.507
If I swap 0 and 1 and then yield and then swap 0 and 2 and then yield,

00:10:39.507 --> 00:10:42.463
it doesn't matter&nbsp;whether that happened because of a recursive call

00:10:42.463 --> 00:10:44.725
or if I just did those things in that order.

00:10:44.725 --> 00:10:45.787
Let's&nbsp;just take a look.

00:10:45.787 --> 00:10:48.246
What's the order that these swaps are happening in

00:10:48.246 --> 00:10:50.682
and that these pushes to the&nbsp;back are happening in?

00:10:50.682 --> 00:10:53.834
Each time a swap occurs or a push to the back occurs,

00:10:53.834 --> 00:10:55.269
I'll just print&nbsp;out a message.

00:10:55.269 --> 00:10:58.784
Here are the swaps and shifts for n equals 5 and r equals 3.

00:10:58.784 --> 00:11:03.112
I also print out&nbsp;a little diagram of where i and j are when they're swapping.

00:11:03.112 --> 00:11:06.636
This way we can visually see which&nbsp;locations are swapping and in what order.

00:11:06.636 --> 00:11:09.896
If you count them, there are five level zero operations.

00:11:09.896 --> 00:11:13.266
Each one surrounded by four level 1 operations.

00:11:13.266 --> 00:11:16.117
Each one surrounded by three level 2 operations.

00:11:16.117 --> 00:11:18.831
Which brings us back to our original counting argument.

00:11:18.831 --> 00:11:23.643
Our innermost recursive call is looping&nbsp;over the three possibilities for this last slot.

00:11:23.643 --> 00:11:27.711
The next recursive call up is looping over the&nbsp;4 possibilities for that slot.

00:11:27.711 --> 00:11:31.457
And the outermost call is looping over the 5 possibilities for&nbsp;the first choice.

00:11:31.457 --> 00:11:34.438
So, what's happening is a kind of weird countdown timer.

00:11:34.438 --> 00:11:37.280
Each column starts with the&nbsp;number of possibilities from the counting argument.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:39.583
So, here we're using 5 4 3.

00:11:39.583 --> 00:11:42.685
Then you start&nbsp;from the right and count down 3 2 1 0.

00:11:42.685 --> 00:11:45.667
When you hit a 0, that means you've tried&nbsp;all the possibilities for the previous column

00:11:45.667 --> 00:11:47.709
and need to switch to the next choice.

00:11:47.709 --> 00:11:50.128
Well, that&nbsp;means there's one less choice for the previous column

00:11:50.128 --> 00:11:52.217
and three more for the next column.

00:11:52.217 --> 00:11:53.779
And then&nbsp;you repeat.

00:11:53.779 --> 00:11:57.012
Eventually, you get to 5 1 3 2 1 0

00:11:57.012 --> 00:12:00.319
At which point, we've used up all the&nbsp;possibilities for that column.

00:12:00.319 --> 00:12:03.062
So, we take the next possibility from the outermost column.

00:12:03.062 --> 00:12:05.361
And then&nbsp;the whole thing starts over on the inner ones.

00:12:05.361 --> 00:12:08.655
We keep going until we're out of choices for&nbsp;the first column and that's where we stop.

00:12:08.655 --> 00:12:13.437
We could completely get rid of our recursive calls&nbsp;if we could just iterate over numbers in this way.

00:12:13.437 --> 00:12:16.125
Okay, that's a much easier task to set our sites on.

00:12:16.125 --> 00:12:19.058
I envision this countdown as kind of like a weird clock mechanism.

00:12:19.058 --> 00:12:20.653
So, I'm going to call these things&nbsp;ticks.

00:12:20.653 --> 00:12:24.123
This is what stores the number of remaining possibilities for each of the items.

00:12:24.123 --> 00:12:26.099
We'll start&nbsp;with the while loop to just keep ticking the clock.

00:12:26.099 --> 00:12:30.755
And then in the loop, when we discover that there&nbsp;are no more possibilities, we'll return.

00:12:30.755 --> 00:12:32.525
We want to take down numbers starting from the right.

00:12:32.525 --> 00:12:34.636
So, we're&nbsp;going to loop over this reversed range.

00:12:34.636 --> 00:12:37.411
Every tick, we subtract 1 and then yield our current state.

00:12:37.411 --> 00:12:41.595
The current state is going to be a tuple of the index that just changed and the current ticks.

00:12:41.595 --> 00:12:45.660
If this were a general purpose function in the library, I would probably make a copy of the ticks&nbsp;here.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:52.069
But because its only purpose is to be used in our permutations algorithm, we don't need to make&nbsp;a copy here.

00:12:52.069 --> 00:12:55.715
We'll just keep in mind that we keep getting back the same copy of ticks mutated.

00:12:55.715 --> 00:12:58.711
After&nbsp;we do a tick, we need to check if we need to do a carry.

00:12:58.711 --> 00:13:01.296
We do a carry if at the current position&nbsp;we hit 0.

00:13:01.296 --> 00:13:05.233
Then we can calculate the number to reset it to based off of its distance from the&nbsp;start.

00:13:05.233 --> 00:13:08.128
When we do a carry, we reset the number back to its original value.

00:13:08.128 --> 00:13:12.677
Then since we did a&nbsp;carry on the next iteration, we need to subtract from one index to the left.

00:13:12.677 --> 00:13:16.179
Well, we're looping from&nbsp;right to left. So, that's what happens automatically.

00:13:16.179 --> 00:13:21.215
So, what we actually need to do is not do that&nbsp;if there wasn't a carry.

00:13:21.215 --> 00:13:25.042
In other words, if there wasn't a carry, then break so that the while will&nbsp;start again.

00:13:25.042 --> 00:13:26.426
And we'll start from the right.

00:13:26.426 --> 00:13:28.583
Then we need to terminate the while-loop somehow.

00:13:28.583 --> 00:13:31.417
We&nbsp;want to stop when there are no more possibilities for the very first location.

00:13:31.417 --> 00:13:33.112
So, when this number&nbsp;hits 0.

00:13:33.112 --> 00:13:34.512
There are a few ways to accomplish this.

00:13:34.512 --> 00:13:38.089
The most obvious way is when there's a carry, just&nbsp;check if you're at index 0.

00:13:38.089 --> 00:13:39.864
And if you are, then it's over.

00:13:39.864 --> 00:13:41.563
Another way to do this is with a&nbsp;for-else.

00:13:41.563 --> 00:13:43.536
If you've never seen for-else before,

00:13:43.536 --> 00:13:48.563
what it does is it executes the else&nbsp;clause if the for loop terminates normally.

00:13:48.563 --> 00:13:51.185
So, if it gets to the end of the range&nbsp;or the range was empty,

00:13:51.185 --> 00:13:52.903
then it will execute this statement.

00:13:52.903 --> 00:13:56.392
If there's a break or an exception&nbsp;thrown, then it won't execute the statement.

00:13:56.392 --> 00:13:59.552
In our case, a normal end of the for-loop means, we tried&nbsp;to carry past 0.

00:13:59.552 --> 00:14:01.077
And so, we should stop there.

00:14:01.077 --> 00:14:05.534
Now, using the iterative countdown function, we&nbsp;can write our iterative permutation function.

00:14:05.534 --> 00:14:08.362
We start off with a yield for the identity&nbsp;permutation.

00:14:08.362 --> 00:14:09.800
Then we start ticking the clock.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:12.193
Here, the index i tells us which index ticked.

00:14:12.193 --> 00:14:15.755
If there's&nbsp;a carry, that's the situation when we need to push the current element to the back.

00:14:15.755 --> 00:14:19.958
Otherwise, we're&nbsp;doing a swap and it's just a quick calculation to compute which index to swap with.

00:14:19.958 --> 00:14:22.381
Fix this little&nbsp;copy-paste error and then we're all set.

00:14:22.381 --> 00:14:24.818
This is a completely iterative permutations algorithm.

00:14:24.818 --> 00:14:28.527
The&nbsp;only thing left to do is error checking and to allow things that aren't lists.

00:14:28.527 --> 00:14:32.205
So, we take in an&nbsp;arbitrary iterable. Just make a copy of it into a tuple,

00:14:32.205 --> 00:14:34.984
Make default parameters and throw errors if&nbsp;anything's wrong.

00:14:34.984 --> 00:14:38.184
Then we just create a list of the same size as our iterable was.

00:14:38.184 --> 00:14:40.779
We use our algorithm&nbsp;to permutate the indices.

00:14:40.779 --> 00:14:43.899
And then we just use the indices to index into our tuple.

00:14:43.899 --> 00:14:46.762
So, basically&nbsp;once you can permute lists, then the whole thing is solved.

00:14:46.762 --> 00:14:49.058
But wait, you say. What about this code?

00:14:49.058 --> 00:14:51.292
Weren't we supposed to understand this code?

00:14:51.292 --> 00:14:53.463
It's finally time. We have all the pieces.

00:14:53.463 --> 00:14:56.193
Now we just&nbsp;need to see them fit together in this function.

00:14:56.193 --> 00:14:59.847
Here's the first side by side. And as you can see, the first part is basically the same.

00:14:59.847 --> 00:15:02.927
Except, they called their variable pool instead of items

00:15:02.927 --> 00:15:04.716
And&nbsp;they're not doing error checking.

00:15:04.716 --> 00:15:08.602
We call a helper function. They just paste it all into the main&nbsp;function.

00:15:08.602 --> 00:15:10.134
So, let's compare the helper functions.

00:15:10.134 --> 00:15:12.783
But first note that whenever the helper function&nbsp;does yield,

00:15:12.783 --> 00:15:16.704
we're always returning a tuple indexed by these indices into the items.

00:15:16.704 --> 00:15:18.063
And same thing&nbsp;on the right side.

00:15:18.063 --> 00:15:23.648
They're always yielding a tuple where they index into their items using the&nbsp;indices.

00:15:23.648 --> 00:15:25.615
They write it this way which is a little bit shorter.

00:15:25.615 --> 00:15:28.381
But it's actually making a&nbsp;copy of the indices every time they yield.

00:15:28.381 --> 00:15:30.591
So, I prefer to do it this way.

00:15:30.591 --> 00:15:34.560
But it's yielding&nbsp;the same things. Now let's compare our subroutine.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:39.214
Well, it doesn't look that similar. It looks like&nbsp;they're doing something different.

00:15:39.214 --> 00:15:41.122
We can sort of correspond some of the parts.

00:15:41.122 --> 00:15:42.992
Here, this is our&nbsp;push to back.

00:15:42.992 --> 00:15:46.551
And this is actually just another way to do push to back.

00:15:46.551 --> 00:15:50.570
And here, we're just swapping&nbsp;items. And here, they're just swapping items.

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:54.000
They're using a negative index. But we're subtracting n&nbsp;from the tick value.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.876
So, it's the same thing as the negative index.

00:15:55.876 --> 00:15:57.795
So, those pieces are basically&nbsp;the same.

00:15:57.795 --> 00:16:02.276
But on the right side, they're doing this whole while for-else with an if and breaks in&nbsp;it.

00:16:02.276 --> 00:16:05.920
Oh, wait. That structure is our countdown code.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:07.782
Which is what we're looping over.

00:16:07.782 --> 00:16:09.557
Take a bit of&nbsp;a closer look at this.

00:16:09.557 --> 00:16:12.334
n is not being modified anywhere in this loop.

00:16:12.334 --> 00:16:17.200
This basically just says:&nbsp;If n is 0, then return. Otherwise, while True.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:22.165
In our countdown code, r ticks. That's the same&nbsp;definition as their cycles.

00:16:22.165 --> 00:16:27.783
And we have a while True with the exact same for-else return and&nbsp;breaks inside.

00:16:27.783 --> 00:16:34.731
And if you look closely, all this swapping and moving with the indices is completely&nbsp;independent of the iteration.

00:16:34.731 --> 00:16:39.476
None of the looping conditions, the n or the for i in range r, none&nbsp;of that depends on the indices.

00:16:39.476 --> 00:16:41.200
It only depends on the cycles.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:43.381
So, we really have the exact same&nbsp;thing.

00:16:43.381 --> 00:16:47.813
We have a while True. Then we have a for i in reversed range.

00:16:47.813 --> 00:16:50.042
We subtract 1 from our tick.

00:16:50.042 --> 00:16:53.775
We check if the tick is 0 and reset it if it is. Otherwise, we break.

00:16:53.775 --> 00:16:56.306
And if we didn't break and&nbsp;we get to the end, then we return.

00:16:56.306 --> 00:16:57.950
That's exactly our countdown code.

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:00.960
The only difference is&nbsp;that we factored it out into a function.

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.564
There we have it. This is functionally identical&nbsp;code.

00:17:03.564 --> 00:17:06.701
The only difference is that we factored out a function or two.

00:17:06.701 --> 00:17:10.165
Yes, it's technically more&nbsp;efficient if you don't have extra function calls.

00:17:10.165 --> 00:17:14.029
But remember, this is just expositional code.&nbsp;This isn't actually performance code.

00:17:14.029 --> 00:17:17.117
The real code was written directly as a C extension&nbsp;module.

00:17:17.117 --> 00:17:19.247
So, that's the end of our analysis of the code.

00:17:19.247 --> 00:17:21.517
And I just want to take a moment to step&nbsp;back.

00:17:21.517 --> 00:17:25.908
This code teaches us a few very important lessons that I hope you can learn from.

00:17:25.908 --> 00:17:33.177
Firstly,&nbsp;if you're considering authoring code that's very clever like this, then you need to be extremely&nbsp;careful.

00:17:33.177 --> 00:17:39.660
If you do something too clever, there's a decent chance that no one else that looks&nbsp;at the code will even attempt to understand it.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:43.650
This really highlights the importance of&nbsp;breaking your code up into consumable chunks.

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:47.920
If they had just factored out this little&nbsp;piece, the countdown into a separate function,

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:52.156
way more people would attempt to understand it. And&nbsp;way more people would understand it.

00:17:52.156 --> 00:17:58.880
Mentally, it's easy to separate the operations that you're doing&nbsp;on the list from this countdown ticking thing.

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:01.316
They're completely separate independent things.

00:18:01.316 --> 00:18:03.622
So, don't mix them together in code like this.

00:18:03.622 --> 00:18:09.403
And second lesson: Don't be afraid to try to&nbsp;solve the problem yourself from first principles.

00:18:09.403 --> 00:18:13.317
Beyond using the most basic version of writing&nbsp;a recursive algorithm,

00:18:13.317 --> 00:18:16.277
none of these steps were really that big of a leap.

00:18:16.277 --> 00:18:19.138
We started by&nbsp;just counting solutions that was easy.

00:18:19.138 --> 00:18:22.179
We wrote the recursive algorithm. That's probably&nbsp;the hardest part.

00:18:22.179 --> 00:18:25.471
But if you have some experience doing that, it's not that much of a leap.

00:18:25.471 --> 00:18:28.392
It's&nbsp;the first and most straightforward algorithm that came up.

00:18:28.392 --> 00:18:34.296
Then we leveraged our recursive&nbsp;implementation to see what it was doing in terms of indices and create an iterative version.

00:18:34.296 --> 00:18:39.375
In my&nbsp;mind, all this work combined is less work than just starting with this finished answer

00:18:39.375 --> 00:18:41.781
and trying&nbsp;to understand what it's doing.

00:18:41.781 --> 00:18:45.843
The whole thing felt very natural, building and discovering the&nbsp;algorithm from scratch.

00:18:45.843 --> 00:18:48.855
But going backwards, it's still confusing to look at.

00:18:48.855 --> 00:18:52.080
So, even when a problem&nbsp;looks hard, don't be afraid to give it a shot.

